        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Matthew</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;The light of the body is the eye: if therefore thine eye be single, thy whole body shall be full of light&#x27; — Cyril reads ophthalmos haplous as undivided intention; the single eye is the heart aimed at one Lord. Tewahedo Christian-anthropology preserves the eye-as-soul-lamp metaphor in monastic catechesis.</p></aside>',
