        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Matthew</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;No man can serve two masters&#x27;: Cyril extends the binary beyond mammon to every rival lordship — possessions, reputation, kin-loyalty when they compete with God. The divided heart is its own first punishment, because integrity is the soul&#x27;s native condition. Tewahedo monastic vow-discipline names this verse as the foundation of single-hearted renunciation.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Matthew (fragments), preserved in J.A. Cramer, Catenae Graecorum Patrum in Novum Testamentum, Vol. I: In Evangelia S. Matthaei et S. Marci (Oxford: University Press, 1840). PD. Greek text also in PG 72 cols. 365-474.",
