        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Matthew</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Fear not them which kill the body, but are not able to kill the soul: but rather fear him which is able to destroy both soul and body in hell&#x27; — Cyril marks the radical re-ordering of fear. Phobēthēte de mallon (fear rather him) re-targets the disciple&#x27;s fear from human persecutors to the divine eschatological judgment-power. The geenna (Gehenna) is named explicitly. Tewahedo eschatology preserves the Cyrillian psychosomatic-judgment unity: soul and body answer together.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Matthew (fragments), preserved in J.A. Cramer, Catenae Graecorum Patrum in Novum Testamentum, Vol. I: In Evangelia S. Matthaei et S. Marci (Oxford: University Press, 1840). PD. Greek text also in PG 72 cols. 365-474.",
