        "Tewahedo — Cyril of Alexandria",
        "Cyril of Alexandria (430).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Cyril of Alexandria</strong> <em>Commentary on Matthew</em> <small>(c. 430 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Jerusalem, Jerusalem, that killest the prophets… how often would I have gathered thy children&#x27;: Cyril marvels at the divine lament. The impassible Word weeps over the unwilling city in his assumed humanity; the lament is real because the human nature is real and entire. This verse anchors Cyrillian impassible-passion against any docetic reading.</p></aside>',
        "Cyril of Alexandria, Commentary on Matthew (fragments), preserved in J.A. Cramer, Catenae Graecorum Patrum in Novum Testamentum, Vol. I: In Evangelia S. Matthaei et S. Marci (Oxford: University Press, 1840). PD. Greek text also in PG 72 cols. 365-474.",
