        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>De Incarnatione Verbi §56</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;When the Son of man shall come in his glory, and all the holy angels with him, then shall he sit upon the throne of his glory&#x27; — Athanasius marks ho Huios tou anthrōpou en tē doxē autou (the Son-of-Man in HIS glory) as the eschatological-RETURN-IN-GLORY Christology. The glory is autou (his-own); the angels accompany (met&#x27; autou pantes hoi angeloi); the throne is tou doxēs autou (of-his-glory). All three predicates are divine: only YHWH has-his-own-glory, is-attended-by-all-angels, and sits-on-the-throne-of-glory. Mt 25:31 is the eschatological-counterpart to Acts 17:31 (Christ as appointed-Judge). Tewahedo iconography of the Pantocrator-on-the-throne is iconographically-anchored at Mt 25:31.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, De Incarnatione Verbi (On the Incarnation), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 25 (1857).",
